    "relation": "cites_only",
    "tranche": "T6",
    "tranche_reason": "The study is full text and discusses scaling, but its own experiments concern robotic task success under different vision encoders and action representations—not language-model held-out cross-entropy, power-law fits, or compute-optimal allocation.",
    "gate_note": "The paper summarizes and cites language-model scaling laws, but reports no tests of their loss curves, extrapolation, or compute-optimal split. Its own results measure LIBERO manipulation success as encoder quality and codebook capacity vary. These are related scaling results in a different construct, recorded under fits_no_version.",
    "test_reason": "The paper cites Kaplan and Hoffmann in one related-work sentence, so the claim is only cited. Its own results are LIBERO-10 robot-policy success rates under different vision encoders, action representations, two policy sizes and three codebook sizes. It measures no language-model loss and fits no power law. Two model sizes do not test the power-law form in another modality (V5) or downstream predictability of language models (V6).",
    "authors_conclusion": "Correct. One addition: the authors say larger codebooks partially recover encoder sensitivity (the key case is |V|=1920), and they argue that scaling in Physical AI means locating information bottlenecks rather than uniformly increasing model or data size.",
    "recipe": "different",
    "recipe_differences": "The study trains and evaluates robotic manipulation policies on LIBERO-10, measuring peak task success under encoder and codebook variations. It does not fit held-out language-model loss against parameters, tokens, or compute.",
    "method_sensitivity": "Reported robotic task success varies with action representation, vision encoder, policy size, and OAT codebook configuration; OAT's codebook-size response is explicitly non-monotonic.",
    "fits_no_version": [
      {
        "finding": "On LIBERO-10, ResNet-18 to SigLIP raises DP success by +21.2 points (M) and +26.0 (L), but OAT by only +3.6 (M) and +10.4 (L). DP rises monotonically over four encoders (36.4 to 63.8). OAT fluctuates (53.8, 57.4, 44.2, 51.0). The encoder effect in OAT changes non-monotonically with codebook size (+3.6, +15.2, +4.0).",
        "proposed_claim": "In visuomotor policies, gains from a better vision encoder depend on the action representation and on discrete codebook capacity. This is not a language-model loss scaling law.",
